<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castrum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as castra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A castrum (plural castra) was a Roman military camp or fortress, laid out on a standard rectangular plan with gates and intersecting main streets. Permanent castra housed the legions along the frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Many modern European cities grew directly from Roman castra or administrative centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castrum.jpg" alt="Castrum" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Animators on behalf of Rotherham Museums and Archives · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templeborough_Roman_Fort_visualised_3D_flythrough_-_Rotherham.webm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
